.TH CCOL_DYNMQ_SEND_ZC 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynmq_send_zc \- send a message to a dynamic queue (no block)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_dynmq_send_zc(ccol_dynamic_queue *dq, c_message_t *msg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function puts the message at the tail of the dynamic queue and returns
immediately. The queue has no size limit, so this call never waits for free
space. It is the only send entry point of the dynamic queue. There is no
send that blocks and no send with a timeout.

On success, the ownership of msg->data moves to the queue. The function
sets msg->data to NULL as the record of that move. The caller must not
free, read, or otherwise use that pointer after the call. Later, a receiver
takes the message out with ccol_dynmq_recv_zc(3),
ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc(3), or ccol_dynmq_timed_recv_zc(3). That receiver
becomes the owner and is the one that frees the data. The library copies no
bytes at any point. Only the pointer and its size move.

On every failure, msg stays exactly as the caller gave it. The caller still
owns msg->data and must free it or send it somewhere else.

A message with msg->data == NULL and msg->size == 0 is valid, and it
carries no payload. This is the usual way to send a wake-up sentinel or an
end-of-stream sentinel through the queue.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq   Dynamic queue to send to
msg  Message to send. On success the ownership of msg->data moves to the
     queue.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            - The function put the message into the queue.
                          msg->data is now NULL, and the queue owns what
                          it pointed to.
ccol_invalid_args       - dq or msg is NULL. Or msg->data is NULL while
                          msg->size is not zero. Or msg->data is not NULL
                          while msg->size is 0.
ccol_not_permitted      - A ccol_dynmq_disable_sending() call turned sends
                          off
ccol_container_full     - The queue already holds ccol_max_elem_count
                          messages
ccol_not_enough_memory  - The function could not allocate the list node
                          for the message
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function never blocks. It returns immediately with a success code or
an error code.
A send that succeeds wakes a blocked ccol_dynmq_recv_zc() receiver or a
blocked ccol_dynmq_timed_recv_zc() receiver. It also wakes one
read-direction waiter on this queue. Such a waiter is a ccol_select()
call, a ccol_select_timed() call, or an ccol_event_loop registration.
The function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_dynmq_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_timed_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_disable_sending (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_msg_count (3)
